GSC data shows legacy SERP traffic and backlinks point to /p-c/{slug}-pilates/ URLs (e.g. /p-c/cankiri-pilates/, /p-c/sivas-pilates/) which currently fall through to the catch-all route and return soft 404s. New rich content lives at /p-c/{slug}. This PR adds three 301 redirect patterns before the final next() call:

- /p-c/{slug}-pilates -> /p-c/{slug}
- /p-c/{city}/{nbhd}-pilates -> /p-c/{city}/{nbhd}
- /p-n/{slug}-pilates -> /p-n/{slug}

Trailing slashes are already normalized earlier in the chain, so these patterns match the post-normalization path. All other logic (WP legacy redirects, city redirects, neighborhood redirects, wp-admin guards, author/tag redirects) is unchanged.

Expected impact: recovers ~1k+ monthly impressions currently hitting soft 404s and consolidates link equity to the canonical city/neighborhood pages.

