Assistant: ## Root cause

`UIShellRouter` *is* declared correctly:

```swift name=mercantis core/UIShell/NavigationShell.swift url=https://github.com/KevinBusuttil/mercantis.core.app/blob/main/mercantis%20core/UIShell/NavigationShell.swift#L10-L29
@MainActor
final class UIShellRouter: ObservableObject {
    @Published var selectedSection: NavigationSection? = .home
    @Published var setupDestination: SetupDestination = .overview
    ...
}
```

The compiler isn't really saying the class is missing `objectWillChange` — it's complaining because the class is `@MainActor` isolated but `ObservableObject` (and its `objectWillChange` requirement) is **non‑isolated**. Under Swift 6 / strict concurrency, an actor‑isolated type cannot satisfy a non‑isolated protocol requirement, so the conformance is rejected with the misleading message:

> Type 'UIShellRouter' does not conform to protocol 'ObservableObject'

(The error fires at line 13 of `mercantis_coreApp.swift` because that's the first place the conformance is actually used — `@StateObject` requires `T: ObservableObject`.)

## Fix

Drop the class‑level `@MainActor` and put it on the methods/init that actually need it (or rely on the fact that `@StateObject`, `App`, and `View.body` are already MainActor‑isolated, which means everything in this router runs on the main actor anyway):

```swift name=mercantis core/UIShell/NavigationShell.swift
final class UIShellRouter: ObservableObject {
    @Published var selectedSection: NavigationSection? = .home
    @Published var setupDestination: SetupDestination = .overview

    func showSetupOverview() {
        selectedSection = .setup
        setupDestination = .overview
    }

    func openNewDocType() {
        selectedSection = .setup
        setupDestination = .newDocType
    }

    func openVisualBuilder() {
        selectedSection = .setup
        setupDestination = .visualBuilder
    }
}
```

### Alternatives if you want to keep `@MainActor`

1. Mark only the mutating methods, not the class:
   ```swift
   final class UIShellRouter: ObservableObject {
       @MainActor func showSetupOverview() { ... }
   }
   ```
2. Provide a non‑isolated `objectWillChange` explicitly:
   ```swift
   @MainActor
   final class UIShellRouter: ObservableObject {
       nonisolated let objectWillChange = ObservableObjectPublisher()
       ...
   }
   ```
   …but you then lose the automatic `@Published` wiring to `objectWillChange`, so option 1 (or just removing `@MainActor`) is cleaner.

